# SHSAT ELA: Reading Comprehension, informational texts (NYS Next Generation ELA
# standards 7R1-7R9 for informational text: evidence, central idea, development of ideas,
# word meaning, structure, the author's position). Every passage is original.


def paras(*ps):
    return "\n\n".join(f"**{i + 1}** {p}" for i, p in enumerate(ps))


STIMULI = {
    "RI1": ("An Old Neighbor Returns", paras(
        "When Henry Hudson sailed into New York Harbor in 1609, the water held something that today's New Yorkers would hardly believe. Oyster reefs covered huge stretches of the harbor floor. For the next two centuries, oysters were so plentiful that street carts sold them the way vendors sell pretzels today.",
        "That plenty did not last. People harvested oysters faster than the reefs could grow back, and by the early 1900s, sewage and factory waste had made the harbor too dirty for the few that remained. Health officials closed the last oyster beds. For most of the twentieth century, the harbor's reefs were gone.",
        "In recent decades, the water has slowly grown cleaner, and a group called the Billion Oyster Project has set out to bring the oysters back. Its goal is to restore one billion live oysters to the harbor by 2035. The work is part science and part teamwork. Restaurants donate the shells left over from oyster dinners. Volunteers clean and cure the shells, and baby oysters, called spat, attach themselves to the old shells and begin to grow.",
        "Why go to so much trouble for a creature most people will never eat? A single adult oyster can filter dozens of gallons of water a day, removing tiny particles as it feeds. A reef also gives fish, crabs and other animals places to hide and find food. Some scientists think that large reefs could even soften the force of storm waves before they reach the shore.",
        "Many of the people doing this work are teenagers. Students at a public high school on Governors Island raise oysters, test the water and dive to check on the reefs. For them, the harbor is not just a view from a ferry. It is a living classroom, and the return of an old neighbor is a sign that a damaged place can heal.")),
    "RI2": ("The Colors Hiding in a Leaf", paras(
        "Every autumn, the hills of upstate New York turn red, orange and gold, and visitors drive for hours to see them. It is natural to think that trees \"paint\" their leaves new colors in the fall. In fact, some of those colors were in the leaves all summer long.",
        "A green leaf is green because of chlorophyll, a substance that captures energy from sunlight so the tree can make sugar. In summer there is so much chlorophyll that it hides other substances in the leaf, including yellow and orange ones called carotenoids. Carotenoids are the same pigments that make carrots orange.",
        "As autumn days grow shorter, many trees stop making new chlorophyll. The chlorophyll already in the leaves breaks down, and the green fades. The yellows and oranges that were there all along finally show through. Nothing new has been added; something has simply been taken away.",
        "Reds and purples are a different story. Some trees, such as red maples, produce new red pigments, called anthocyanins, in the fall. These pigments form when sugar builds up in the leaf. That is why a stretch of sunny days followed by cool nights, which keeps sugar trapped in the leaves, often brings the brightest reds.",
        "Eventually, a layer of cells at the base of each leaf stem seals the leaf off from the branch, and the leaf drops. The tree rests through the winter, living on the food it stored. In spring it will grow new leaves full of chlorophyll, and the yellows and oranges will hide again until next fall.")),
    "RI3": ("In Defense of Doing Nothing", paras(
        "Ask a group of seventh graders what they did last weekend, and many will list activities: practice, lessons, tutoring, a birthday party, homework. Ask when they last had an afternoon with nothing planned, and the room may go quiet. Many families work hard to keep children busy. I would like to argue for the opposite: every young person needs regular time to be bored.",
        "Boredom feels unpleasant, and that is exactly why it is useful. When there is nothing to do, the mind starts looking for something. A child stuck indoors on a rainy day may build a fort out of couch cushions, invent a game with a deck of cards or finally open the book that has been sitting on the shelf. No adult planned those activities. The child came up with them.",
        "Busy schedules can crowd out that kind of thinking. A student who moves from school to soccer to piano to homework rarely has to decide what to do next; someone else has already decided. There is nothing wrong with soccer or piano. The problem comes when every hour is filled, leaving no room for a young person to discover what he or she actually enjoys.",
        "Some parents worry that unplanned time will turn into hours of scrolling on a phone. That concern is fair. Screens can fill empty time so quickly that boredom never has a chance to do its work. But the answer is not to schedule every minute. It is to set aside some time that is free of both activities and screens.",
        "None of this requires money or special equipment. It requires only a willingness to let an afternoon stay empty and to resist the urge to fill it. A young person who learns to handle boredom learns something no lesson can teach: how to make an interesting life out of whatever is at hand.")),
    "RI4": ("A Scientist of Many Talents", paras(
        "On September 12, 1992, the space shuttle Endeavour lifted off from Florida with seven astronauts aboard. One of them, Dr. Mae Jemison, became the first African American woman to travel into space. For Jemison, the flight was not the start of a dream but the latest step in a life spent following many interests at once.",
        "Jemison grew up in Chicago, where she spent hours at the library reading about science and astronomy. She also loved to dance. At sixteen she entered Stanford University, where she earned a degree in chemical engineering and also studied African American history. Later she earned a medical degree from Cornell University.",
        "Before joining NASA, Jemison worked as a doctor for the Peace Corps in Sierra Leone and Liberia, in West Africa. She cared for volunteers, wrote guides on health and helped with medical research. The work taught her to make careful decisions far from home, a skill that would matter in space.",
        "NASA selected Jemison as an astronaut in 1987. On her mission of about eight days, she served as a science specialist and ran experiments on how the body responds to life without gravity, including studies of bone loss and motion sickness. She also carried small objects that honored people and places she cared about, among them a poster from a dance company.",
        "After leaving NASA, Jemison started a technology company and created an international science camp for students. She has often said that science belongs to everyone and that creativity in the arts and creativity in science come from the same place. Her own life, which has included medicine, engineering, dance and space flight, may be the best support for that idea.")),
    "RI5": ("The Animal That Refuses to Quit", paras(
        "Under a microscope, a drop of water squeezed from a clump of moss may reveal a strange little animal lumbering along on eight stubby legs. It is a tardigrade, also known as a water bear. Most tardigrades are less than a millimeter long, but they are famous among scientists for being nearly impossible to kill.",
        "Tardigrades live almost everywhere: in moss on city sidewalks, in mud at the bottom of lakes, on mountaintops and in the deep sea. Like fish, they need a film of water around them to stay active. What makes them remarkable is what they do when that water disappears.",
        "When its home dries out, a tardigrade pulls in its head and legs and curls into a tiny, shriveled ball called a tun. Its body loses almost all of its water, and its activity slows so much that it can barely be measured. In this state, a tardigrade can survive for years. When water returns, it can swell up and begin crawling again within hours.",
        "In the tun state, tardigrades have survived conditions that would destroy most living things, including freezing cold, intense heat and high doses of radiation. In 2007, scientists sent dried tardigrades into orbit on a satellite and exposed them to outer space. Some of them survived and later produced healthy young.",
        "Researchers are now studying how tardigrades protect their bodies while dried out. Some have found special proteins that seem to hold the parts of a cell in place, a little like packing material around a fragile package. Learning how these proteins work might someday help scientists store medicines without refrigeration. A creature most people have never seen could end up solving very human problems.")),
    "RI6": ("What the Meadows Do Not Show", paras(
        "Each year, millions of people stroll, jog and picnic in Central Park. Its winding paths, meadows and lakes look so natural that many visitors assume the land was always this way. In fact, nearly every hill, pond and stretch of woods in the park was planned.",
        "In the 1850s, New York City was growing fast, and its crowded streets had few green spaces. City leaders decided to build a large public park, and in 1858 they chose a design called the Greensward Plan, by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux. The two men wanted a place where people of every background could escape the noise of the city. Workers moved huge amounts of rock and soil, planted trees by the thousands and dug lakes where there had been swamps.",
        "But the land was not empty before the park was built. Hundreds of people lived there, many of them in a community called Seneca Village. Most of its residents were African American, and many owned their homes, which at the time could give Black men the right to vote in New York. The village had churches and a school.",
        "In 1857, the city took the land for the park and forced the residents to leave. They were paid for their property, but many believed the payments were too low, and the community was scattered. For more than a century, Seneca Village was nearly forgotten.",
        "In recent years, historians and archaeologists have worked to tell its story. Digs in the park have uncovered pieces of dishes, shoes and other objects from village homes, and signs now mark the place where the village stood. The park is still a gift to the city. Its history, however, is more complicated than its peaceful meadows suggest.")),
    "RI7": ("Hunting by Ear", paras(
        "On a summer evening, a bat swoops over a pond, twists in midair and snatches a moth that a person could barely see. It does all this in near darkness. Many bats find their food not mainly by looking but by listening.",
        "These bats use a skill called echolocation. As a bat flies, it sends out rapid, high-pitched calls, most of them too high for human ears to hear. The sound waves spread out, strike objects ahead and bounce back as echoes. By listening to those echoes, the bat can tell where an object is, how far away it is and even how it is moving.",
        "As a bat closes in on an insect, it speeds up its calls, sometimes to more than a hundred a second. The faster calls give it a sharper picture of its target in the final moments of the chase. Scientists call this burst of sound a feeding buzz.",
        "Not every bat works this way. Many fruit bats, which eat fruit and nectar, rely mainly on their large eyes and keen sense of smell. Their food stays still, so they have less need to track fast-moving targets in the dark.",
        "Some moths have evolved defenses against echolocation. Certain kinds can hear bat calls and dive toward the ground when a bat approaches. Others make clicking sounds of their own that seem to confuse or warn hunting bats. In the dark skies over a pond, a quiet contest between hunter and hunted plays out every night.")),
}

